      "content": "I see what you mean about the \"safe bet\" in Zone A. It's basically saying that because they have the resources and the structure, they're more \"worthy\" of the aid because it's harder to lose. But if we follow that logic, aren't we essentially punishing the communities that are already struggling? It feels like we're just reinforcing the existing gap—sending more to those who have it and leaving those in Zone B or D to fend for themselves because they're \"too risky.\" If we want to be \"efficient,\" doesn't that naturally lead to us favoring the white, affluent areas just because they're easier to work with?",
